Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The asynchronous HTTP method view class inherits from the synchronous HTTP method dispatch base class because the base class already supports asynchronous methods through Flask\'s `ensure_sync` mechanism, eliminating the need for a separate implementation.\\n\\nIn `src/flask/views.py`, the `MethodView` class (lines 138-192) overrides `dispatch_request()` to route HTTP requests to method-specific handlers. At line 191, it uses `current_app.ensure_sync(meth)(**kwargs)` to invoke the handler method. The `ensure_sync` method (defined in `src/flask/app.py` at lines 966-978) automatically detects whether a function is a coroutine and wraps it using `async_to_sync` if needed, which uses the `asgiref` library to run async functions in an event loop.\\n\\nThis design means `MethodView` already handles both synchronous and asynchronous method handlers transparently. When `AsyncMethodView` (defined in `tests/test_async.py` at line 30) inherits from `MethodView` and defines `async def get()` and `async def post()` methods, the inherited `dispatch_request()` method automatically routes to these async methods and `ensure_sync` ensures they execute correctly.\\n\\nBy inheriting rather than implementing independently, `AsyncMethodView` reuses:\\n1. The method discovery logic in `__init_subclass__` (lines 165-180) that automatically sets the `methods` attribute based on defined handlers\\n2. The HEAD request fallback logic (lines 187-188) that routes HEAD to GET if no HEAD handler exists\\n3. The integration with Flask\'s view registration system through the inherited `as_view()` method from `View`\\n\\nThe architectural constraint is that Flask\'s async support runs each async view in a thread with an event loop (as documented in `docs/async-await.rst` lines 36-45), meaning each request still ties up one WSGI worker. This limits true concurrency compared to ASGI frameworks, but the inheritance design allows async methods to work seamlessly within Flask\'s existing WSGI architecture without requiring a separate implementation."}' + '\n')
